Q: string() function doesn&#39;t work in document.evaluate A: <pre><code>document.evaluate(&quot;string(//@id)&quot;, document, null, XPathResult.STRING_TYPE, null)
</code></pre>
<p>Replace <code>ORDERED_NODE_SNAPSHOT_TYPE</code> with <code>XPathResult.STRING_TYPE</code>, since <code>string()</code> returns a string, not a node-set. Then get the value using <code>.stringValue</code>:</p>
<pre><code>const result = document.evaluate(&quot;string(//@id)&quot;, document, null, XPathResult.STRING_TYPE, null);

console.log(result.stringValue);
</code></pre>

Q: Memoize a function which uses instance attributes A: <p>Considering <code>a, b, c</code> are hashable.</p>
<p>First, We can also cache the <code>some_computation_inside</code> to reduce redundant compute time.</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>import functools

@functools.cache
def some_computation_inside(a, b, c):
    print(&quot;miss cache&quot;)
    return f&quot;{a} {b} {c}&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Then, if <code>some_long_computation1</code> and <code>some_long_computation2</code> are deterministic calls based on the class state, if you want to reduce the time spent on those methods, consider making the class a frozen dataclass and make those functions cached properties. Thanks to the frozen property of the object, internal state won't be altered between calls. Without, the cache would be incorrect.</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>import dataclasses
import functools


@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class Spam:
    some_attribute: str = &quot;spam&quot;

    def output(self):
        output_string = some_computation_inside(
            self.some_long_computation1, 
            self.some_long_computation2,
            self.some_attribute,
        )
        return output_string

    @functools.cached_property
    def some_long_computation1(self):
        return self.some_attribute.upper()

    @functools.cached_property
    def some_long_computation2(self):
        return self.some_attribute.capitalize()
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; s = Spam()
&gt;&gt;&gt; s.output()
miss cache
SPAM Spam spam
&gt;&gt;&gt; s.output()
SPAM Spam spam
</code></pre>
<p>If you need to mutate the object and invalid the cache, forget about frozen. What you need is to detect mutation of your object and invalidate cache when it happens. This helper class will do the trick:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>import functools


class PropertiesCacheInvalidation:
    @classmethod
    @functools.cache
    def _cached_property_names(cls):
        return frozenset(
            name
            for klass in cls.__mro__
            for name, attr in vars(klass).items()
            if isinstance(attr, functools.cached_property)
        )

    def __setattr__(self, key, value):
        super().__setattr__(key, value)
        for name in self._cached_property_names():
            self.__dict__.pop(name, None)
</code></pre>
<p>Now, if you inherit from this helper class, your cached properties will have their cache invalidated on object mutation. Here is qn example:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>import dataclasses
import functools


@dataclasses.dataclass
class Spam(PropertiesCacheInvalidation):
    some_attribute: str = &quot;spam&quot;

    def output(self):
        output_string = some_computation_inside(
            self.some_long_computation1,
            self.some_long_computation2,
            self.some_attribute,
        )
        return output_string

    @functools.cached_property
    def some_long_computation1(self):
        return self.some_attribute.upper()

    @functools.cached_property
    def some_long_computation2(self):
        return self.some_attribute.capitalize()
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; s = Spam()
&gt;&gt;&gt; s.output()
miss cache
SPAM Spam spam
&gt;&gt;&gt; s.output()
SPAM Spam spam
&gt;&gt;&gt; s.some_attribute = &quot;eggs&quot;  # Here, cache is invalidated
&gt;&gt;&gt; s.output()
miss cache
EGGS Eggs eggs
&gt;&gt;&gt; s.output()
EGGS Eggs eggs
</code></pre>
